WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:07.529 --> 00:00:12.129
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; May we then move on please
to item 5, which is the review of maternity

00:00:12.129 --> 00:00:17.560
services and implementing the National Maternity
Review. Jane, please would you introduce the

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:20.669
paper for us.
Jane Cummings&gt;&gt; Thank you very much, Chair.

00:00:20.669 --> 00:00:26.800
What I plan to do is just take the Board through
where we are with the review, some of the

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:33.690
key findings, and our proposals for how we
intend to implement them. As the Board will

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:37.649
know, we commissioned Baroness Cumberlege
last year to undertake the review of maternity

00:00:37.649 --> 00:00:44.239
services. That was part of the five-year forward
plan that we had published. That review was

00:00:44.239 --> 00:00:51.359
published on 23rd February this year. I think
one of the key things to identify and highlight

00:00:51.359 --> 00:00:57.780
is that this review was done on behalf of
all of the ALBs, and the implementation of

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:03.170
that review is something that we will all
be contributing to. Although NHS England will

00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:08.350
be taking the lead on that, it is absolutely
imperative and vital that we work with and

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:12.710
alongside NHS Improvement, Health Education
England, the Department of Health and so on

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:18.520
in order to deliver it.
The findings were really interesting, and

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:22.130
one of the key things that they came up with
was that they actually found maternity services

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:28.490
are now safer than they have ever been, and
satisfaction continues to grow, which is really

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:35.270
important. We are also able to show that stillbirth,
neonatal and maternal deaths had improved.

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:40.700
However, part of the review was doing a detailed
review of quality, which Bill Kirkup led,

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:46.310
and that did show some continuing variation,
and unacceptable variation, in care across

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:52.149
the country. So one of the key priorities
for us will be to improve that level of variation,

00:01:52.149 --> 00:01:56.079
to help everybody get to be the very best
they can.

00:01:56.079 --> 00:02:00.840
The other issue that I think is important
that we note is that our population is changing;

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:06.469
the age of first-time mothers is increasing.
That means that many of those mothers have

00:02:06.469 --> 00:02:13.320
other long-term conditions, for example diabetes.
We also have more complex care, and again,

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:19.260
that has an impact on how we implement the
review findings.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:26.209
Two of the areas were particularly interesting.
One was that we talk a lot – and we have

00:02:26.209 --> 00:02:32.090
talked a lot – about choice, about the importance
of balancing safety and informed choice for

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:38.140
women, and a significant number of women do
deliver in NHS obstetric units, despite the

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:43.280
fact that when you talk to them, they often
say that they would prefer to give birth in

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:50.069
a midwifery-led unit or perhaps at home. We
do not always balance what women tell us they

00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:57.920
want with the reality of that, so the review
was very clear about wanting to give women

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:04.989
much more choice and enable them to make informed
choices, but to do that in a very safe way.

00:03:04.989 --> 00:03:09.870
The other point that I find particularly interesting
was that many of the healthcare professionals

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:17.670
to whom the review spoke talked about some
issues with teamworking, maybe some relationship

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:22.860
issues between, for example, obstetricians,
neonatologists and midwives, and that was

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:28.090
a really interesting link to what Bill Kirkup
found in the Morecambe Bay Review, so there

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:33.260
was a repetition of some of the issues that
came out of that. Again, some of the recommendations

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:36.769
and some of the key themes are based on trying
to address those.

00:03:36.769 --> 00:03:42.819
I will not go through the vision and the review
details, because they are there and Board

00:03:42.819 --> 00:03:47.510
colleagues will have read them. Just to pick
up a couple of key ones. The first one is

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:54.769
around personalised care, and a very strong
emphasis on enabling women to have care that

00:03:54.769 --> 00:03:59.209
is centred around their own particular views
and needs, that they have an ability to make

00:03:59.209 --> 00:04:06.290
that choice, that we provide them with opportunities
to test out a personal maternity care budget

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:11.310
that enables them to look at where they want
to have their antenatal, intra-partum, post-partum

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:15.780
care.
We also need to look at providing continuity.

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:20.500
Again, if you talk to women, one of the things
that is most important to them is that they

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:24.620
actually know their midwives, that they are
able to have a relationship with that midwife

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:30.470
that follows them through the time that they
are pregnant and into birth, and subsequently

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:37.030
after birth. The reality of that is quite
difficult to deliver; it is not that straightforward

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:41.720
to have somebody available for the whole of
a woman’s pregnancy, so we are looking to

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:47.509
pilot having four to six midwives who maybe
look after a group of women. I think that

00:04:47.509 --> 00:04:53.289
will actually address what some of the issues
around patient experience may be, but it is

00:04:53.289 --> 00:05:00.180
important to the women that we serve.
There is a very clear overlap with the Mental

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:07.000
Health Review. The work on postnatal depression,
perinatal mental health, was absolutely critical,

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:15.090
and so the overlap and the synergy between
those two areas, so the two different programmes

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:19.789
of work, is really important. I think completely
independently they both came up with the same

00:05:19.789 --> 00:05:25.020
issues, and I think that just highlights the
importance that we are placing on that.

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:28.940
Health Education England will be working with
us and colleagues around multi-professional

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:35.009
working, and really trying to do specific
training and education with colleagues working

00:05:35.009 --> 00:05:40.400
together so that we do not have separate education
for doctors and midwives; that we actually

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:47.350
start to do that together, and we really truly
look at breaking down some of the organisational

00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:54.270
boundaries and working across those boundaries
to really wrap services around women.

00:05:54.270 --> 00:06:01.319
In terms of governance and our implementation,
we plan to develop an integrated approach

00:06:01.319 --> 00:06:06.880
to that with a single implementation board.
That will have representation from all of

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:13.590
the different ALBs. Each of the ALBs will
take a lead on a specific area, so a good

00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:19.560
example of that will be when we talk about
the workforce and education, HEE will clearly

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:25.169
take the lead on that. NHS England will clearly
take the lead on choice, for example, and

00:06:25.169 --> 00:06:30.660
NHS Improvement will take the lead on safety,
because as the Board knows, the patient safety

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:36.460
team have transferred to NHS Improvement as
of tomorrow.

00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:41.069
The paper also sets out the various different
workstreams that we are going to look at,

00:06:41.069 --> 00:06:46.259
and I think each of those workstreams has
the beginnings of a detailed implementation

00:06:46.259 --> 00:06:50.880
plan alongside those, and we are also talking
at the moment to Public Health England about

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:55.470
whether we should have a particular workstream
on public health and prevention. We know from

00:06:55.470 --> 00:07:04.259
experience that the opportunity to improve
public health and the prevention agenda is

00:07:04.259 --> 00:07:11.440
really critical during pregnancy. You are
talking to and working with people who are

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:16.780
genuinely very keen to have the very best
outcome for their babies. You can have an

00:07:16.780 --> 00:07:21.550
opportunity to influence a whole family, and
so there is something, a really good opportunity,

00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:28.539
for us to highlight that health and wellbeing
gap in the prevention agenda, as we work and

00:07:28.539 --> 00:07:33.259
implement this.
Our expectation is that we have the first

00:07:33.259 --> 00:07:39.530
of the implementation programme boards towards
the end of April, into May, and I am very

00:07:39.530 --> 00:07:44.039
happy to come back to the Board with more
detailed implementation plans, and we will

00:07:44.039 --> 00:07:49.590
provide that with the oversight and governance
that we have for all of our major programmes.

00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:53.860
I am quite excited about this. I think it
gives us a fantastic opportunity to improve

00:07:53.860 --> 00:08:00.360
the care for women and their babies, and I
think the report gives us a great deal of

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:05.599
scope to do that over the next five years,
and I commend the report to the Board and

00:08:05.599 --> 00:08:07.539
I would be grateful for any comments. Thank
you very much.

00:08:07.539 --> 00:08:11.870
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; Thank you, Jane. Let us pick
up that invitation to the Board for any comments

00:08:11.870 --> 00:08:16.669
on the report.
Moira Gibb&gt;&gt; Can I just ask Jane really about

00:08:16.669 --> 00:08:22.639
the impact of geography? Because it seems
to me that we have recognised that in lots

00:08:22.639 --> 00:08:28.280
of places, with a national drive, as it were,
there is an opportunity for consistency. However,

00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:34.570
sometimes there is the opposite, that you
cannot get something that is designed for

00:08:34.570 --> 00:08:43.790
a particular geography. Is it local solutions
or is it national?

00:08:43.790 --> 00:08:51.580
Jane Cummings&gt;&gt; That is a really helpful question,
Moira. The review specifically looked at geography,

00:08:51.580 --> 00:08:56.870
in particular some of the more geographically
isolated areas, and that was partly done as

00:08:56.870 --> 00:09:03.570
a result of the Morecambe Bay inquiry. The
review is also very clear that actually, although

00:09:03.570 --> 00:09:06.830
there is a national framework and national
guidelines, the only way that you are going

00:09:06.830 --> 00:09:12.670
to be able to do this is through local adaptation,
and so the vast majority of this can be done

00:09:12.670 --> 00:09:18.870
locally; we just might need to shift and slightly
alter how it is done.

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:23.280
One of the key issues will be the relationships
with wider networks, so one of the recommendations

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:28.890
looks at creating a wider network of maternity
services, both from a commission and a provider

00:09:28.890 --> 00:09:32.710
point of view, and actually the best way of
doing that is in geographical locations, and

00:09:32.710 --> 00:09:37.800
so we would have the opportunity to share
and use different types of technology in order

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:43.100
to support women in different parts of the
country. So national oversight and national

00:09:43.100 --> 00:09:49.160
recommendations, but local adaptation and
local review.

00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:56.770
David Roberts&gt;&gt; As somebody who has observed
this for the last couple of years, it strikes

00:09:56.770 --> 00:10:01.410
me that making progress on mental health has
proved to be one of the hardest things that

00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:07.990
we have been faced with in terms of demonstrably
making change for the better. One of the absolute

00:10:07.990 --> 00:10:14.380
critical recommendations here is an improvement
in the service, perinatal, postnatal mental

00:10:14.380 --> 00:10:22.260
health, and therefore I would be interested
in knowing what we are going to do differently,

00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:28.590
and is there actually the skill and capacity
and capability locally to deliver what is

00:10:28.590 --> 00:10:40.370
required? If not, what are we going to do?
Jane Cummings&gt;&gt; We have some good examples

00:10:40.370 --> 00:10:46.980
of that. We have examples of midwives who
have been specifically trained in perinatal

00:10:46.980 --> 00:10:52.400
mental health who have been doing some really
positive work, and in fact we were only discussing

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:59.230
that this week with a consultant who has been
leading a lot of that. In addition, when we

00:10:59.230 --> 00:11:05.400
created the additional health visitor workforce
that we worked on over the last few years

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:09.640
and completed last year, one of the very key
high impact actions that those health visitors

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:13.200
were trained to do was to look at perinatal
mental health. So we increased the number

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:20.250
of health visitors across England by a significant
amount, to at the time over 4,000, and I think

00:11:20.250 --> 00:11:28.050
that is also helping. Part of the recommendations
also talk about having a longer follow-up

00:11:28.050 --> 00:11:35.800
care for women postnatally, and particularly
for higher-risk women, for them to have access

00:11:35.800 --> 00:11:40.930
to both midwifery and then subsequently health
visitor support for maybe six weeks, as opposed

00:11:40.930 --> 00:11:49.020
to the current midwives for a few days. That
will provide some consistency and some continuity.

00:11:49.020 --> 00:11:54.130
I do not know whether Bruce wants to add anything
more from the Mental Health Review, but they

00:11:54.130 --> 00:11:58.570
will lead that part of the implementation.
Bruce Keogh&gt;&gt;I do not have a huge amount to

00:11:58.570 --> 00:12:05.420
add, other than to say that it is also a priority
in the Mental Health Review, and we have a

00:12:05.420 --> 00:12:12.290
team working on that. I think there is a bigger
issue though, David, and that is how we integrate

00:12:12.290 --> 00:12:17.120
our mental health endeavour into all of the
major reviews that we are doing; so it is

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:23.080
important in all the long-term conditions,
it is important in our cancer review, and

00:12:23.080 --> 00:12:27.350
it is obviously important in the maternity
review, and we are cognisant of that and trying

00:12:27.350 --> 00:12:31.810
to bring a coherence of approach across all
of them.

00:12:31.810 --> 00:12:42.230
John Burn&gt;&gt; As the proud grandfather of two
home water births, which I was very impressed

00:12:42.230 --> 00:12:48.390
by, although no-one mentioned how hard it
is to empty the damn thing afterwards, I have

00:12:48.390 --> 00:12:52.560
been thinking very much about the safety issue
and about diversification of personal choice.

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:57.480
One of the questions that I would just like
to expand on is this question of technology

00:12:57.480 --> 00:13:02.510
and information sharing, because obviously
if we are moving women away from obstetric

00:13:02.510 --> 00:13:08.870
units, then sharing data and record-sharing
is ever more critical. We have said that we

00:13:08.870 --> 00:13:14.070
are committed to the development of a digital
portal; that does not give us a good timescale.

00:13:14.070 --> 00:13:17.580
Do you have a feel for how long it will be
with the work with the National Information

00:13:17.580 --> 00:13:22.530
Board before we have a digital portal?
Jane Cummings&gt;&gt; I cannot tell you that now,

00:13:22.530 --> 00:13:28.250
but we do have a plan to do that, and it is
part of the work that the NIB are looking

00:13:28.250 --> 00:13:33.210
at. I do not have the details off the top
of my head, but I can certainly come back

00:13:33.210 --> 00:13:38.640
and let you have that. What we do know though
is that the work we have done so far and some

00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:45.320
of the early adopters that we will look at
will actually start to really test that out

00:13:45.320 --> 00:13:50.230
and get that up and running, to show the positive
impact that it can have.

00:13:50.230 --> 00:13:54.470
Karen Wheeler&gt;&gt; Just to reinforce that, we
do not have a timetable for that yet, but

00:13:54.470 --> 00:14:03.040
that is part of the plan that we are pulling
together now and we allocate the National

00:14:03.040 --> 00:14:07.800
Information Board funding associated with
the various domains, and this is clearly a

00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:12.580
significant priority that we will be looking
to take forward, with Jane’s help, obviously,

00:14:12.580 --> 00:14:17.270
and under that domain.
Jane Cummings&gt;&gt; One of the issues about choice

00:14:17.270 --> 00:14:23.380
is enabling women to have a really informed
choice, so that they understand some of the

00:14:23.380 --> 00:14:30.540
risks involved, but also making sure that
there are very good agreed pathways, so that

00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:36.790
people can flit from potentially a home birth
or a midwifery-led unit birth, into something

00:14:36.790 --> 00:14:41.870
that actually requires maybe more intensive
support, or an obstetric unit, and that that

00:14:41.870 --> 00:14:47.600
is a very clearly set out, agreed pathway
for women and their carers, so they know what

00:14:47.600 --> 00:14:53.870
the issues are and they know how that can
happen. That is really important. Choice is

00:14:53.870 --> 00:14:59.440
vital, but safety is absolutely critical,
and we need to be able to marry the two together,

00:14:59.440 --> 00:15:04.130
and that is one of our key priorities.
Victor Adebowale&gt;&gt; I just welcome the report.

00:15:04.130 --> 00:15:09.080
Really good stuff. As the former president
of the Health Visitors Association, I welcome

00:15:09.080 --> 00:15:13.640
the increase in health visiting numbers. I
was just curious about a couple of things.

00:15:13.640 --> 00:15:18.680
First of all, not all women are the same,
and I am particularly keen on what you might

00:15:18.680 --> 00:15:26.660
have to say about the most vulnerable women,
the women least likely to make choices. I

00:15:26.660 --> 00:15:32.180
am just curious about where the energy in
the report is around that.

00:15:32.180 --> 00:15:38.300
The second thing is, I note that the Mental
Health Taskforce is taking some leadership

00:15:38.300 --> 00:15:44.520
around the mental health issue. What I am
interested in is, who is holding the ring

00:15:44.520 --> 00:15:52.010
locally? While I note your point about health
visitors being trained, it is how the mental

00:15:52.010 --> 00:15:58.630
health system takes up that role. Do we have
thoughts about who might be doing that locally?

00:15:58.630 --> 00:16:02.930
Because I think in the past, what has happened
is women have fallen between two stools, so

00:16:02.930 --> 00:16:10.600
I am curious about that. Do we have thoughts
about who is picking that up locally; who

00:16:10.600 --> 00:16:14.600
is in charge, effectively?
Jane Cummings&gt;&gt; I think the point we have

00:16:14.600 --> 00:16:19.760
made, and one of the recommendations we have
made, is that there should be these networks.

00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:24.880
The other issue we have done is we have just
issued invitations for those who want to pilot

00:16:24.880 --> 00:16:29.820
the choice work, and we are going to have
early adopters that we are going to start

00:16:29.820 --> 00:16:34.690
to look at for later in the year. I think
the proposal will be that through those early

00:16:34.690 --> 00:16:39.480
adopters, we actually are able to demonstrate
and test what different local models might

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:45.540
look like in order to address that. Part of
the review certainly did concentrate on looking

00:16:45.540 --> 00:16:55.340
at access and choice options to everybody
and not just a certain cohort, and it is back

00:16:55.340 --> 00:17:01.760
to this issue about looking at the population,
almost segmenting the population that we have

00:17:01.760 --> 00:17:06.900
and actually making sure that the recommendations
are applicable to everybody and not just a

00:17:06.900 --> 00:17:11.680
certain group.
The other important area I think is highlighting

00:17:11.680 --> 00:17:19.310
that, by having Bill Kirkup as part of the
review team and leading the quality part of

00:17:19.310 --> 00:17:24.910
the review team, and having key colleagues
who were involved in the Morecambe Bay inquiry

00:17:24.910 --> 00:17:30.900
and the report, the links between the two
– I will not use the word ‘synergy’

00:17:30.900 --> 00:17:37.330
again – were there for a very specific reason:
to try to make sure that we did ensure that

00:17:37.330 --> 00:17:41.660
this report and this review did look at the
outcomes of that, too.

00:17:41.660 --> 00:17:48.850
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; Good. Well, thank you very
much, Jane. I think the Board notes the recommendations

00:17:48.850 --> 00:17:53.840
and will give full endorsement to the high-level
approach for implementation that you are proposing.

00:17:53.840 --> 00:17:59.330
Could I just suggest one thing? The governance
arrangements will have an implementation board

00:17:59.330 --> 00:18:03.870
reporting to the Five Year Forward View programme
board. I would not wish this Board to lose

00:18:03.870 --> 00:18:08.380
sight of what is going on, so perhaps in a
year’s time, if we could have a brief update

00:18:08.380 --> 00:18:12.470
and an understanding of how the implementation
has worked through, we would be grateful for

00:18:12.470 --> 00:18:12.970
that.
Jane Cummings&gt;&gt; Absolutely. I consider that

00:18:12.970 --> 00:18:15.760
to be one of my priorities, so yes.
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; Okay, thank you very much

00:18:15.760 --> 00:18:16.140
for that. May we then turn to item 6 on the
agenda, which is the NHS England Corporate

00:18:16.140 --> 00:18:16.330
and NHS Performance Report? Karen, I think
speaking on behalf on this occasion of Richard

00:18:16.330 --> 00:18:16.830
Barker.

